From 95ff2f5d25a2aa120be1f0739801ec70a2b26f28 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <florian.stosse@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:31:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [e-acsl] translate both operands of <==> once, without copies

Since a9a91e590f, the translation of `p1 <==> p2` outside interlang rewrote
the predicate as `(p1 ==> p2) && (p2' ==> p1')`, where `p1'` and `p2'` are
deep copies, so that the RTE analysis would see each occurrence. The labels
pre-analysis runs once on the whole file and records labelled terms by
identity, so it knows nothing about the copies. When an operand contains a
labelled term (`\old`, `\at`, or a formal in a postcondition, which the
kernel reads as `\old`), the translation of the copy aborted with:

  labels pre-analysis was not performed on construct \old(b)

for instance on `ensures (\result != 0) <==> (b->n == 0);`.

In the rewritten predicate, both operands are evaluated whatever their
values: `p1` in the first conjunct, and `p2` either in the first conjunct
(when `p1` holds) or in the second one (when it does not). So translate
`p1` and `p2` once each, in this order, and return `!e1 == !e2`, as the
interlang translation of `Piff` already does with its two operands. Each
operand still gets its RTE guards, once, and no copy is needed.

The new test `constructs/iff_labelled_terms.c` has a postcondition with
`\old`, a postcondition with a formal, and an assertion with
`\at(..., Pre)`. In `memory/oracle/gen_objects.0.c`, the two `<==>` checks
of `objects.c` (`ARR` and `AM`) are translated the new way: each operand,
with its RTE guards, is computed once before the comparison, instead of
twice in two nested implications. No other oracle changes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
